==== Infrared Spectrometer ====
[[File:Mariner 6 and 7 UV spectrometer.png|thumb|Ultraviolet Spectrometer diagram]]
The Infrared [[Spectrometer]] scanned [[Infrared|infrared wavelengths]] to detect signatures of atmospheric gases and surface materials. It used multiple spectral channels to measure emission and reflection in the 1.9 to 14.3 [[Micrometre|μm]] range.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite book |last=Collins |url=https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19730005151/downloads/19730005151.pdf |title=THE MARINER 6 AND 7 PICTURES OF MARS |date=1971 |publisher=NASA |pages=20}}</ref>

==== Ultraviolet Spectrometer ====
The Ultraviolet Spectrometer (UVS), built by the [[University of Colorado]] [[Laboratory for Atmospheric and Space Physics|Laboratory for Atmospheric and Space Physics (LASP)]]<ref name="Pearce1971" />, measured [[ultraviolet]]  emission spectra between 110 nm to 340 nm from Mars' [[upper atmosphere]] to determine its composition and structure.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mission |url=https://lasp.colorado.edu/mariner6_7/ |access-date=2026-01-26 |website=Mariner 6 & 7 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=UVS |url=https://lasp.colorado.edu/instruments/uvs/ |access-date=2026-01-28 |website=Laboratory for Atmospheric and Space Physics |language=en-US}}</ref> 

==== Infrared Radiometer ====
The Infrared [[Radiometer]] measured [[thermal infrared]] radiation using multiple spectral channels. It consisted of two {{cvt|1|in|adj=on}} telescopes, one covering the wavelength range of 8.1 μm to 12.5 μm and the other the range of 17.9 μm to 25.1 μm.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Neugebauer |last2=Münch |date=October 1971 |title=Mariner 1969 Infrared Radiometer Results: Temperatures and Thermal Properties of the Martian Surface |url=https://scispace.com/pdf/mariner-1969-infrared-radiometer-results-temperatures-and-3nxza9npo1.pdf |journal=The Astronomical Journal |volume=76 |issue=8 |pages=719 |doi=10.1086/111189 |bibcode=1971AJ.....76..719N }}</ref>

==== Celestial Mechanics Experiment ====
The [[Celestial mechanics|Celestial Mechanics]] experiment used [[Doppler effect|Doppler shifts]] from the spacecraft's radio signal to refine knowledge of Mars' gravitational field, orbital parameters, and spacecraft trajectory.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |date=2017-12-20 |title=Mariner 6 - NASA Science |url=https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mariner-6/ |access-date=2026-01-26 |language=en-US}}</ref>

==== S-Band Occultation Experiment ====
This experiment used the spacecraft's [[S band|S-band]] radio signal as it passed behind Mars ([[occultation]]) to probe the Martian atmosphere.<ref name=":2" />

==== Conical Radiometer ====
This instrument measured incoming thermal radiation from the environment and helped characterize the spacecraft's thermal conditions in flight.<ref name=":2" /> 

